{-
   The standard library the ownership resolver is built against, and nothing else.

   `compiler/resolve_mir` cannot yet compile `lib/Core`, and a stage with no library at all cannot
   compile a program either: `doc/spec/ownership.md`'s three classes are asked about every type in
   every program, and `[a]` is grammar rather than a call, so what it means has to be reachable
   without being written. This directory is the smallest library that answers both.

   It is a **library, not a mechanism.** The compiler finds it exactly as it finds `lib/` - the same
   walk, the same `Core/Core.yana` test, the same `-lib` override - and every declaration in it binds
   to the compiler the same way `lib/` does, by writing `@builtin(role)` where a role is claimed. So
   nothing here is a stand-in the compiler knows about; it is Core, written small.

   ## What this is for, and how it ends

   Every declaration is a *subset* of the one `lib/Core` writes, with the same name, the same head
   and a signature the real one would accept. Reading the two side by side is the whole of the
   maintenance: `lib/Core/Core.yana` is the source of truth and this file is what fits through the
   stage today.

   It ends by deletion. When `-resolver ownership` compiles the real library, `libraryPath` stops
   defaulting here, this tree goes, and no compiler code changes - which is the property that makes
   it worth having rather than a set of synthesized declarations in C++.
-}

{-
   The operator fixities - `lib/Core/Core.yana`'s own block, copied number for number.

   **What an operator does and how it groups are two statements with two sources**, and this is the
   second one. What `+` computes is `compiler/resolve_mir/resolve_expr.cpp`'s table, which stands in
   for `Num.add` until an operator can dispatch to a class member; how far `+` reaches is a
   *declaration*, looked up through the imports like any other name, and there is nowhere else for it
   to come from.

   It used to be a column in that table as well, and the two disagreed. `shl` sat one rung above
   `and` there and beside it here, so `a and b shl c` grouped as `a and (b shl c)` under this stage
   and `(a and b) shl c` under the one that reads the real Core - the same source, two programs, and
   nothing to say so. The column is gone and this is what replaced it, which is the only arrangement
   in which the question has one answer.

   The subset is the operators this stage resolves. Core also declares `+=` and its four siblings,
   `??`, `++`, `rol` and `ror`; those are functions this stage has no table row for, and a fixity for
   an operator nothing can call would be a grouping for an expression nobody can write. They arrive
   with the declarations they group.

   Every one is `infixl`, exactly as Core writes it - `doc/spec/expressions.md`, and the reason the
   climb needs no associativity column to be *correct* today rather than merely untested.
-}
infixl 1 ||
infixl 2 &&
infixl 3 ==
infixl 3 !=
infixl 3 >
infixl 3 >=
infixl 3 <
infixl 3 <=
infixl 5 `xor`
infixl 5 `or`
infixl 6 `and`
infixl 6 `shl`
infixl 6 `shr`
infixl 6 `sar`
infixl 7 +
infixl 7 -
infixl 8 *
infixl 8 /
infixl 8 %

{-
   The names ordinary arithmetic is written in - `lib/Core/Core.yana`'s own six, verbatim.

   Nothing about these needed a compiler change: `alias` is an ordinary declaration in this stage,
   `resolveAliasTargets` walks them, and a type name reaches `findAlias` before it reaches
   `findBuiltin`. They were simply not written down, so every fixture said `I32` where the language
   says `Int`.

   **`Int` is a name for a width and not a width of its own**, which is what makes it an alias rather
   than a row in `primitive.def`: a dump prints `I32` for both, because the alias is transparent and
   there is one type behind the two spellings.

   Core also declares `Size`, `USize` and `CodeUnit`, and those are *not* here and could not be -
   they are widths the target answers rather than names for a fixed one, so `primitive.def` declares
   them and this file has nothing to add.
-}
pub alias Int = I32
pub alias Long = I64
pub alias UInt = U32
pub alias ULong = U64
pub alias Float = F32
pub alias Double = F64

{-
   `Bool` - `lib/Core/Core.yana`'s own declaration, attribute and all.

   An **ordinary enum**, which is the whole point of it being here: the compiler used to create this
   type itself, on the argument that a comparison has to answer something and this stage might have
   no declarations to answer with. A library it can compile is what removes the argument. What the
   compiler keeps is the *representation* - a two-constructor enum manifests as one byte holding a
   tag, and that is the same answer the builtin gave.

   `@builtin(boolean)` requires the two constructors to be numbered from zero, and this is - which
   makes `False` the zero the comparisons answer with and `True` the one. The words are the
   library's; `compiler/intrinsic` holds neither of them.
-}
@builtin(boolean)
pub data Bool = False | True

{-
   The three ownership classes - `doc/spec/ownership.md`.

   They were synthesized in C++ until this file existed, for the reason that made this file: the
   classifier asks about every type in every program, so a fixture that declares no instance and
   imports nothing still needs them to exist. Written here they are ordinary declarations, checked by
   the ordinary rules, and `instance Drop(T)` in a program is compared against a class rather than
   against a shape test.

   `@builtin` is what binds each to the compiler, and the spelling is `known.def`'s enumerator. The
   real `lib/Core/Core.yana` writes exactly these three attributes on exactly these three classes.
-}
@builtin(reclaim)
pub class Reclaim(a):
    fn reclaim(->value: a) -> {}

@builtin(drop)
pub class Drop(a):
    fn drop(->value: a) -> {}

@builtin(copy)
pub class Copy(a):
    fn copy(from: a) -> a

{-
   What an unadorned literal is - `lib/Core/Core.yana`'s two conversion classes, heads and all.

   `1` has no width of its own and `1.5` has no precision of its own, so what each *is* where no
   position asks for anything is a statement the language makes here, in a **parameter default**.
   The compiler used to write the two answers out itself, and the decimal one disagreed with this
   file: `let x = 1.5` was an `F64` under this stage and an `F32` under the one that reads the real
   library. Reading the declaration is the only arrangement in which the question has one answer.

   The members are the conversions themselves and nothing calls them yet - a literal at a declared
   type is a constant of that type here rather than a `fromInt` call. They are written because the
   class is the library's, and a subset that dropped its member would be a different class.
-}
@builtin(fromInt)
pub class FromInt(a = Int):
    fn fromInt(value: Long) -> a

@builtin(fromDecimal)
pub class FromDecimal(a = Float):
    fn fromDecimal(value: Double) -> a

{-
   The growable array - `[a]`'s other spelling, and the type a window is a window *into*.

   `doc/spec/collections.md`: "`[a]` and `Array(a)` are one type, as `[k:v]` and `Map(k, v)` are".
   So the bracket form has to resolve to a declaration, and this is it; `@builtin(array)` is what
   binds it, and `lib/Core/Array.native.yana` writes the same attribute on the real one.

   **The fields are the library's and the compiler reads none of them.** What the compiler decides
   about this type is the *window* - a shared or unique `[a]` in a binding position, whose two words
   are a manifestation and not a type - and a window points into whatever the owner holds. The real
   Core says `{run: Run(a), length: Count}` natively and `{items: %a, length: @host Count}` on JS,
   which is the same statement made twice with a different first word; this is the JS shape, because
   it is the one that needs no `Run` and no allocator to be written down.

   Nothing can build one yet: there is no allocator in this stage, so an *owned* `[a]` has no
   producer and every occurrence of this type is a window. `newArray`, `push` and the rest arrive
   with the heap.
-}
@builtin(array)
pub data Array(a) {items: %a, length: Size}

{-
   `length(xs)` - what a container's extent is, and the first operation on one.

   Core declares it over `Chunked(c, a)` and this declares the one instance of that shape which needs
   no class: a **window**, which is what every container adapts to. A `[T *n]` argument adapts at the
   call and the extent it adapts with is its own count, so `length` on a fixed array is that number
   and no load - see `compiler/intrinsic/op.def`, where the emitter's side of that is written.

   `@builtin(sequenceLength)` is what binds it; the compiler holds no name for it, so what the
   library calls it is the library's to choose.
-}
@builtin(sequenceLength)
pub fn length(xs: [a]) -> Size
